// One 384-bit constant, two names, two files, and nothing saying they must agree.
//
// `issues/system/0349a` found this by hashing function bodies rather than comparing names, and
// called it the sharpest single item in a sweep of 39 duplicated bodies. Checked limb by limb here
// and it holds exactly:
//
//     packages/bls/src/fp12.wac:214   Fp2 frob12C1_3()   /** ξ^((p^3−1)/6), the Fp12 Frobenius
//                                                            coefficient on w. */
//     packages/bls/src/g2.wac:158     Fp2 psiY()         /** 1/ξ^((p−1)/2), its y-coefficient. */
//
//     a[0] = 0xa55c9ad1; a[1] = 0x3e2f585d; …   identical, all twelve limbs
//     b[0] = 0x5aa30fda; b[1] = 0x7bcfa7a2; …   identical, all twelve limbs
//
// Twenty-four hand-transcribed hex words, twice, on the **signature-verification path**. Nothing
// imports the other, nothing asserts they are equal, and no test would notice if one were corrected
// and the other were not.
//
// ## It is not a copy-paste mistake, which is what makes it interesting
//
// The two doc comments describe genuinely different expressions — a Frobenius coefficient on `w` in
// the twelfth-degree tower, and the y-coefficient of the ψ endomorphism on G2. They are the same
// field element because of an identity in the tower, and **neither comment mentions the other**.
//
// So the duplication is a *naming* decision and a defensible one: each file calls the value by the
// role it plays there, and importing `frob12C1_3` into `g2.wac` to serve as `psiY` would put a name
// from the wrong layer into the reader's way. Deduplicating by deleting one is the wrong fix.
//
// The right one costs nothing and nobody did it, because the two names live in two files that have
// no reason to read each other:
//
//     // g2.wac
//     import { frob12C1_3 } from "./fp12.wac";
//     /** 1/ξ^((p−1)/2), its y-coefficient — **the same element** as the Fp12 Frobenius
//      *  coefficient on w, by ξ^((p³−1)/6) = 1/ξ^((p−1)/2) in this tower. */
//     Fp2 psiY() { return frob12C1_3(); }
//
// One value, both names, and the identity written where a reader of either meets it. That is the
// whole fix and it needs no language feature — which is the finding, because the thing that would
// have produced it is not a type. It is somebody hashing the bodies.

import { Fp } from "./fp.wac";

/**
 * An element of Fp2, as a pair of Fp coefficients.
 *
 * **Declared here because this directory has no tower file**, and that is a placement this file
 * would not choose. `./fp.wac` exports `Fp` and `Canonical` and stops; the real package builds Fp2,
 * Fp6 and Fp12 above it, and `Fp2` belongs with them. Writing it here keeps the import graph honest
 * — the alternative was `import { Fp2 } from "./fp.wac"`, which the resolver check refused because
 * `fp.wac` does not export it — and it is the fourth time today a type has landed in the wrong file
 * because the right one is not written yet.
 */
export const struct Fp2 { Fp c0; Fp c1; }

/**
 * ξ^((p³−1)/6) — the Fp12 Frobenius coefficient on `w`, and by an identity in this tower also
 * 1/ξ^((p−1)/2), the y-coefficient of ψ on G2.
 *
 * **Both names for it are correct and this file holds one value.** `./fp12.wac` and `./g2.wac`
 * import it and each states the identity in the doc comment a reader of that file will meet.
 */
export const Fp2 XI_FROB_3 = Fp2( … );

// ## What could not be written
//
// ### Nothing relates two constants that must be equal, and `const` does not help
//
// The obvious language answer is *make them constants* — and `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *828
// constants are written as functions* wants exactly that. It does not touch this. Two `const`
// declarations of one value in two files are two constants that can drift, the same as two
// functions. `const` fixes how a constant is spelled, not how many of them there are.
//
// What would relate them is an assertion — `frob12C1_3() == psiY()` in a test — and that is
// available today and absent. So this is not a language gap at all, which is worth saying plainly
// in a directory whose job is to find them: **the sharpest defect in a 39-body duplication sweep
// wants a test, not a feature.**
//
// The nearest thing that *is* a language question is narrow and probably not worth it: a way to say
// *this declaration is an alias for that one* without re-exporting it under the wrong name and
// without a wrapper function. wac has no re-export (`[§wac-no-reexport-f7kn4wq]`, specified), so the
// wrapper above is the available form, and a wrapper is fine — one call, folded or not, on a path
// that already does a pairing.
//
// ### A 384-bit constant is twenty-four assignments and a fixed length nothing checks
//
// `u32[12]()` then twelve `a[i] = …` lines, twice per constant, throughout this package. Three
// things at once, each with its own entry above:
//
//   - **a `const` array would replace the twenty-four statements** — available today, and the
//     package uses functions for the same reason 828 other sites do;
//   - **`u32[12]` is not a type**, so nothing says an `Fp2` limb array has twelve words. This is the
//     322-length-checks entry, in the one package where a wrong length is a wrong signature check;
//   - **and a third thing I claimed and then found false**, kept here because the error is the useful
//     part. I wrote that these limbs have no oracle — typed from a paper, caught only by a broken
//     pairing. `packages/bls/test/tower.py` is *"the BLS12-381 field tower in plain Python integers …
//     no Montgomery form, no limbs, no carries … so a bug in its representation cannot also be a bug
//     here"*, and it **computes** the coefficients rather than transcribing them:
//     `FROB12_C1 = [f2pow(XI, (P**i - 1) // 6) for i in range(12)]`. `vectors.py` imports it and
//     generates what the wac tests consume, so a wrong limb disagrees with a value derived from the
//     field definition.
//
// So the duplication is real and the urgency I attached to it was not. What produced the wrong claim
// is worth more than the claim: I grepped the *source* files for an oracle, found none, and did not
// look in `test/`. The two packages this directory has praised for keeping a definitional oracle
// beside the implementation — `gzip`'s `crc32Bitwise`, and now `bls` — differ only in **which
// directory the oracle lives in**, and one of those is visible from the file and the other is not.
